.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH dotman-sync 1  "sync "
.SH NAME
dotman\-sync \- Capture local changes, commit, pull, apply what arrived and push
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdotman sync\fR [\fB\-m\fR|\fB\-\-message\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-local\fR] [\fB\-\-take\-repo\fR] [\fB\-\-forget\-deleted\fR] [\fB\-\-restore\-deleted\fR] [\fB\-\-accept\-identity\fR] [\fB\-\-approve\-hooks\fR] [\fB\-\-approve\-dirs\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-hooks\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-system\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-mass\-delete\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-root\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Capture local changes, commit, pull, apply what arrived and push
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-message\fR \fI<MSG>\fR
Commit message, replacing the generated `sync(<host>): ...` line
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-hooks\fR
Run no hooks
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be captured, committed, pulled, applied and pushed; change nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR
After the pull, also write the system files outside $HOME, through one sudo, doas or run0 call
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
With \-\-system: write the system files without asking
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-mass\-delete\fR
Commit even when the repo changes delete at least half of the tracked files (10 or more); a deleted file under a `[dirs]` entry that $HOME still has is captured again by the next sync, so stop managing those with `forget <dir>` first
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH DECISIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-local\fR
Keep the local version of conflicting and existing files; sync captures it into the repo for every machine, or into the variant this machine applies when that names its host. To keep a file to this machine only, run `add \-\-for host=<host> <file>` instead, or `add \-\-encrypt \-\-for host=<host> <file>` when any of its sources is encrypted
.TP
\fB\-\-take\-repo\fR
Take the repo version of conflicting and existing files, after backing them up
.TP
\fB\-\-forget\-deleted\fR
Stop managing files deleted locally; they stay deleted
.TP
\fB\-\-restore\-deleted\fR
Write files deleted locally back from the repo
.TP
\fB\-\-accept\-identity\fR
Accept a changed machine identity and use the variants it selects
.TP
\fB\-\-approve\-hooks\fR
Approve the current `[hooks]` table on this machine, printing it first, and run its hooks. When the table changed since a warning here showed it, show the new table and approve nothing; the same command again approves it
.TP
\fB\-\-approve\-dirs\fR
Approve on this machine, printing them first, the `[dirs]` entries it has not approved, and capture the new files under them. An entry that `.dotman.toml` gained from elsewhere captures nothing here until approved; `add <dir>` approves the directory it adds
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show verbose output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational messages
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR
Disable pager output
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-root\fR
Allow running as root
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB3\fR
Paths were left undecided or held back, or hooks were not approved:
the [hooks] table changed since a warning showed it, so
\-\-approve\-hooks showed the new table and approved nothing.
Everything else is still committed, pulled, applied and pushed,
except when an uncommitted repo edit holds a credential or an age
key: then sync stops before the pull.
.TP
\fB4\fR
The pull hit a conflict. Sync aborted it, so nothing it brought
reached $HOME, and printed the steps to resolve it.
.TP
\fB1\fR
An error. Among them: the remote shares no commit with the repo, or
the commit would delete at least 10 tracked files and at least half
of them, which needs a yes at the prompt or \-\-allow\-mass\-delete.
The next sync captures again the files under [dirs] that $HOME
still has, unless \fBforget <dir>\fR stops managing them first.
